Apple on Wednesday unveiled <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/10/28/apple-new-macbook-pro/">new 14-inch and 16-inch MacBook Pro models with touch-enabled OLED displays</a>, and more details about them continue to be shared by tech reporters who were invited to Apple's hands-on experience in Los Angeles.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/10/MacBook-Pro-PROLED-XDR-Display.jpg" alt="" width="1600" height="900" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1074684" /><br/>
Nicolas Lellouche of the French website <em>Numerama</em> shared 15 details about the new MacBook Pro models <a href="https://www.numerama.com/tech/2363001-jai-essaye-le-macbook-pro-tactile-en-vrai-et-je-me-pose-beaucoup-de-questions.html">in an article</a> and a <a href="https://x.com/LelloucheNico/status/2115810350180483413">social media post</a>. Some of this information has already surfaced, but he provided a useful summary:<br/>
<br/>
<ul><li>Apple said the keyboard and trackpad remain the primary input methods, with touch input available as an additional option for those who want it.</li><br/>
<li>Apple said there is now a generation of young creators who grew up using touchscreens, so it felt the timing was right to bring the functionality to the Mac.</li><br/>
<li>Apple said music creators prefer touch over trackpads for controlling virtual instruments.</li><br/>
<li>"Traffic light" controls in the top-left corner of macOS windows expand when pressed.</li><br/>
<li>Items in a list become more spaced out when touched.</li><br/>
<li>A swipe up from the bottom of the screen brings up the Dock.</li><br/>
<li>A swipe down from the top of the screen brings up Spotlight/Siri AI.</li><br/>
<li>A long press is equivalent to a right click.</li><br/>
<li>Holding a window and quickly moving it left or right triggers Split View.</li><br/>
<li>If you close your Mac while your finger is touching the display, touch input is temporarily deactivated to prevent accidental touches.</li><br/>
<li>The camera is stabilized so it does not shake on video calls when you touch the screen.</li><br/>
<li>You cannot wake the MacBook Pro by touching the screen.</li><br/>
<li>Multi-touch supports up to 10 fingers at the same time. No word yet on toes (kidding).</li><br/>
<li>Live Activities from iPhone apps can appear in the Dynamic Island.</li><br/>
<li>There are settings that allow you to turn off touch input and the illuminated Apple logo on the MacBook Pro's lid.</li></ul><br/>
<br/>
